cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      # Demo 1: Streaming ZET Vehicle Positions as Vector Tiles


      **HrOUG 2026 · Spatial Meets AI**


      Environment: Oracle Autonomous AI Database 26ai


      This notebook takes the live **ZET GTFS-Realtime** feed of Zagreb's trams
      and buses into the database, indexes the positions in **space and time**
      with the enhanced CBTREE spatial index, and streams them as **Mapbox
      Vector Tiles** through ORDS to a web map.


      **Task:** Find the anwer to question ***"Which ZET stops need shade
      first?"***.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ## Table of Contents

      1. Data Sources
      2. Ingestion Design
      3. Session Setup
      4. Clean Up
      5. Landing and Position Tables
      6. Ingest Procedure
      7. Test with Real and Reconstructed Feed Data
      8. Spatio-temporal CBTREE Index
      9. Spatio-temporal Queries
      10. Vector Tiles
      11. REST Endpoints with ORDS
      12. Retention
      13. Loader Job in the Database
      14. Map with MapLibre
      15. Demo End
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ## 1. Data Sources

      ### ZET GTFS-Realtime (live vehicle positions)

      | Property | Value |
      |---|---|
      | Provider | ZET – Zagrebački električni tramvaj |
      | Endpoint | [https://zet.hr/gtfs-rt-protobuf](https://zet.hr/gtfs-rt-protobuf) |
      | Format | GTFS Realtime `FeedMessage`, Protocol Buffers, `FULL_DATASET` |
      | Content | Trip updates and vehicle positions in one feed |
      | Refresh | About every 10–15 seconds (as polled by third-party apps) |
      | Terms | To be confirmed with ZET. Attribute ZET as the source. |

      **What the feed actually contains** (checked on 2026-10-08 at 11:47 UTC):
      * 945 entities: 587 trip updates and 358 vehicle positions, 13 of them on tram line 6.
      * Filled fields: entity id, vehicle id, trip id, route id, start date, latitude, longitude, timestamp.
      * Not filled: bearing, speed, stop id, vehicle label, current status.

      A third-party app listing describes the feed as marked *test-only* by ZET, and it can lag or lapse.

      Further reading:
      * Transitland feed record: [ZET GTFS-RT](https://www.transit.land/feeds/f-u25k-zagreba%C4%8Dkielektri%C4%8Dnitramvaj~rt)
      * [GTFS Realtime reference](https://gtfs.org/documentation/realtime/reference/)
      * [Protocol Buffers JSON mapping](https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/json/): the loader sends this JSON form of the feed to the database.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ### ZET GTFS Static (schedule)

      | Property | Value |
      |---|---|
      | Endpoint | [https://zet.hr/gtfs-scheduled/latest](https://zet.hr/gtfs-scheduled/latest) |
      | Format | GTFS Schedule (ZIP of CSV files) |
      | Licence | Derived products allowed, attribution required (per [Transitland](https://www.transit.land/feeds/f-u25k-zagreba%C4%8Dkielektri%C4%8Dnitramvaj)) |
      | Role | Route types (tram vs. bus), stop ids, shapes. Loaded in a later step. |

      ### OpenStreetMap: Tram Line 6 (already loaded)

      Loaded by the scripts in this folder:
      * `create_table_tram_lines.sql` and `insert_tram_lines.sql` → `TRAM_LINES`: tram 6 track, one row per direction
      * `create_table_tram_stations.sql` and `insert_tram_stations.sql` → `TRAM_STATIONS`: stops A–D

      Available as GeoJSON via Spatial Studio:
      * [Tram Lines](https://ij1tyzir3wpwlpe-kpadw23ai.adb.eu-frankfurt-1.oraclecloudapps.com/spatialstudio/api/v1/jsonstream/dd88bf69c61da3c4eb3706808d0907ec)
      * [Tram Stations](https://ij1tyzir3wpwlpe-kpadw23ai.adb.eu-frankfurt-1.oraclecloudapps.com/spatialstudio/api/v1/jsonstream/83eea2e3fd1ffeac64189dd3fdf2c750)

      Source relations: [147093 (Črnomerec → Sopot)](https://www.openstreetmap.org/relation/147093) and [2183640 (Sopot → Črnomerec)](https://www.openstreetmap.org/relation/2183640). © OpenStreetMap contributors, [ODbL](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright).
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: |
      --
      -- Check the prerequisites: tram 6 tables from the OSM scripts
      --

      SELECT
        table_name,
        num_rows
      FROM
        user_tables
      WHERE
        table_name IN ('TRAM_STATIONS', 'TRAM_LINES')
      ORDER BY
        table_name;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ## 2. Ingestion Design

      ```
      ZET GTFS-RT feed (protobuf)                   loader outside the database (optional)
        │ ZET_FEED_JOB, every 15 s: ZET_FEED.POLL   │ ORDS POST /zet-ingest/positions (OAuth)
        │ UTL_HTTP + protobuf decoding in PL/SQL    │
        ▼                                           │
      ZET_STREAM.INGEST ◄───────────────────────────┘
        ├─► ZET_FEED_RAW            raw JSON per feed message: replay, reprocessing
        └─► ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS   SDO_GEOMETRY + UTC timestamp, CBTREE index FILTER BY observed_at
              ▲   │
              │   ├─► spatio-temporal SQL queries
              │   └─► SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE ─► ORDS GET /zet/vt/{layer}/{z}/{x}/{y} ─► MapLibre page
              │                                  (alternatives: APEX Map Region, Spatial Studio)
              └── INSERT /*+ APPEND */ ◄── DBMS_CLOUD external table ◄── July 2026 CSV.gz in Object Storage

      ZET_PURGE_JOB, hourly: deletes rows older than 24 hours from both tables
      ```

      Design choices, following the Oracle Database guidance for streamed data:
      * **The database pulls the feed.** The scheduler job `ZET_FEED_JOB` polls the feed every 15 seconds (section 13): `UTL_HTTP` over HTTPS, protobuf decoded in PL/SQL. No external machine or script is needed, only a network ACL for `zet.hr`. A loader outside the database can still POST to the ORDS endpoint (section 11).
      * **Set-based ingest.** One `MERGE` per feed message over `JSON_TABLE`, not row by row. This has the same effect as array DML (`FORALL`, `executemany`).
      * **Raw landing table.** Every new feed message is kept as JSON. That enables replay for the live demo and reprocessing later.
      * **Idempotent.** A feed message with a header timestamp that is already stored is skipped. The primary key `(vehicle_id, observed_at)` plus `MERGE ... WHEN NOT MATCHED` skips positions that repeat between messages.
      * **Bulk load for history.** The reconstructed July 2026 data (1.1 million positions) goes in with one direct-path `INSERT ... SELECT` from a `DBMS_CLOUD` external table, followed by fresh statistics (section 7.2).
      * **UTC everywhere.** GTFS-RT timestamps are POSIX seconds in UTC. Convert to `Europe/Zagreb` only in queries.
      * **Hourly partitions.** Both tables are interval-partitioned by hour on a virtual `DATE` copy of their timestamp, starting at 1970-01-01 (section 5).
      * **Surrogate keys.** Each table has an `ID` identity column with `CACHE 1000`; in `ZET_FEED_RAW` it is the primary key.
      * **Spatio-temporal index.** CBTREE with `FILTER BY observed_at` (Release Update 23.26.3): one index search covers place and time.
      * **Data Lakehouse workload.** `NOCOMPRESS` for the high-frequency DML tables, because the default is Hybrid Columnar Compression. Autonomous AI Database ignores logging, LOB storage and physical attribute clauses, so they are left out. Optimizer hints are ignored by default (see the session setup below).
      * **Retention.** The hourly job `ZET_PURGE_JOB` deletes rows older than 24 hours (section 12). It deletes the July data too, so disable it while you work with July. Dropping hourly partitions would be faster, but needs a `LOCAL` CBTREE index with `FILTER BY`, which is still to be verified.
      * **Serving.** Vector tiles come straight from the tables with `SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE` through ORDS (section 11) and show on a MapLibre page (section 14). APEX Map Regions (vector tile layers from APEX 26.1) and Spatial Studio are low-code and no-code alternatives.
      * **Operations.** Both jobs have a cell to monitor their runs and a commented-out cell to disable them. Section 4 removes every object of the notebook, so it can run again from a clean state.
      * **Security.** The network ACL opens only `zet.hr` on port 443. The ingest endpoint is protected by an ORDS privilege and an OAuth client; the tile endpoints are public and read-only.
      * **More consumers.** If several applications need the stream, add Transactional Event Queues.

      References:
      * [Creating a spatial index (CBTREE, FILTER BY)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/spatl/creating-spatial-index.html)
      * [High-performance spatiotemporal analysis in Oracle AI Database](https://blogs.oracle.com/database/high-performance-spatiotemporal-analysis-in-oracle-ai-database)
      * [New Spatial features in Oracle AI Database 26ai](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/spatl/changes-this-release-oracle-spatial-developers-guide.html)
      * [Autonomous AI Database: call web services](https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/paas/autonomous-database/serverless/adbsb/call-web-services.html)
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Database version: the spatio-temporal CBTREE index needs Release Update
      23.26.3 or later

      --


      SELECT version_full FROM product_component_version;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      ## 3. Session Setup


      The Data Lakehouse workload ignores optimizer hints by default. Enable
      them for this session so `DOMAIN_INDEX_FILTER` takes effect.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: |
      --
      -- Disable ignoring of hints (needed in ADB)
      --

      ALTER SESSION SET OPTIMIZER_IGNORE_HINTS=FALSE;
      ALTER SESSION SET OPTIMIZER_IGNORE_PARALLEL_HINTS=FALSE;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: |
      SET TIMING ON
      SET SERVEROUTPUT ON
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      ## 4. Clean Up


      This section removes every database object the notebook creates, so the
      notebook can run again from a clean state. Run it before section 5. Each
      step skips objects that don't exist yet, so it also works on the first
      run.


      | Object | Type | Created in section |

      |---|---|---|

      | `ZET_PURGE_JOB`, `ZET_FEED_JOB` | Scheduler jobs | 12, 13 |

      | `zet-gtfsrt-loader` | ORDS OAuth client | 11 |

      | `zet.ingest.write` | ORDS privilege | 11 |

      | `zet.ingest`, `zet.tiles` | ORDS modules | 11 |

      | `ZET_FEED`, `ZET_TILES`, `ZET_STREAM` | Packages | 13, 10, 6 |

      | `ZET_TRAM_POSITIONS_JULY_EXT` | External table | 7.2 |

      | `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS` (with index `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS_STIDX`),
      `ZET_FEED_RAW` | Tables | 5, 8 |

      | Spatial metadata of `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS` | Row in
      `USER_SDO_GEOM_METADATA` | 8 (automatic) |


      **Kept:**

      * `TRAM_LINES` and `TRAM_STATIONS`: prerequisites from the OSM scripts

      * the REST-enabled schema

      * the network ACL for `zet.hr` (granted by ADMIN, section 13)


      **The OAuth client is removed too.** The next run of section 11 issues a
      new client id and secret; update `.credentials` then.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Clean up 1/3: scheduler jobs and ORDS objects

      -- Dynamic calls, so each step is skipped if the object (or the API) does
      not exist

      --


      DECLARE
        PROCEDURE step (p_name IN VARCHAR2, p_block IN VARCHAR2) IS
        BEGIN
          EXECUTE IMMEDIATE p_block;
          DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('removed: ' || p_name);
        EXCEPTION
          WHEN OTHERS THEN
            DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('skipped: ' || p_name || ' (' || SQLERRM || ')');
        END step;
      BEGIN
        step('scheduler job ZET_FEED_JOB',     q'[BEGIN DBMS_SCHEDULER.DROP_JOB(job_name => 'ZET_FEED_JOB', force => TRUE); END;]');
        step('scheduler job ZET_PURGE_JOB',    q'[BEGIN DBMS_SCHEDULER.DROP_JOB(job_name => 'ZET_PURGE_JOB', force => TRUE); END;]');
        step('ORDS client zet-gtfsrt-loader',  q'[BEGIN ORDS_SECURITY.DELETE_CLIENT(p_name => 'zet-gtfsrt-loader'); END;]');
        step('ORDS privilege zet.ingest.write', q'[BEGIN ORDS.DELETE_PRIVILEGE(p_name => 'zet.ingest.write'); END;]');
        step('ORDS module zet.ingest',         q'[BEGIN ORDS.DELETE_MODULE(p_module_name => 'zet.ingest'); END;]');
        step('ORDS module zet.tiles',          q'[BEGIN ORDS.DELETE_MODULE(p_module_name => 'zet.tiles'); END;]');
        COMMIT;
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Clean up 2/3: packages, tables (the spatial index is dropped with its
      table) and spatial metadata

      --


      DROP PACKAGE IF EXISTS zet_feed;

      DROP PACKAGE IF EXISTS zet_tiles;

      DROP PACKAGE IF EXISTS zet_stream;

      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS zet_tram_positions_july_ext PURGE;

      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS zet_vehicle_positions PURGE;

      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS zet_feed_raw PURGE;


      DELETE FROM user_sdo_geom_metadata WHERE table_name LIKE 'ZET_%';

      COMMIT;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Clean up 3/3: check that nothing is left (both queries return no rows)

      --


      SELECT object_type, object_name

      FROM user_objects

      WHERE object_name IN ('ZET_FEED', 'ZET_TILES', 'ZET_STREAM',
      'ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS', 'ZET_FEED_RAW',
                            'ZET_TRAM_POSITIONS_JULY_EXT', 'ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS_STIDX', 'ZET_PURGE_JOB', 'ZET_FEED_JOB')
      ORDER BY object_type, object_name;


      SELECT name, uri_prefix

      FROM user_ords_modules

      WHERE name LIKE 'zet.%';
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ## 5. Landing and Position Tables

      * `ZET_FEED_RAW` keeps every received feed message as native JSON.
      * `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS` holds one row per vehicle and timestamp, with the position as `SDO_GEOMETRY` (WGS84, SRID 4326).

      Both use 26ai features: `IF NOT EXISTS`, annotations (usable by Select AI later) and `DEFAULT ON NULL`.

      **Built for streaming large data volumes.** Based on the Oracle AI Database skills and the 26ai documentation, for Autonomous AI Database Serverless with the Data Lakehouse workload:

      | Choice | Why |
      |---|---|
      | Hourly interval partitions on a virtual `DATE` column (`received_date`, `observed_date`), starting at 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC | A new partition appears automatically for each hour that receives data, a whole hour can be removed at once, and bulk loads write direct-path into their own partitions. The start is the epoch of the GTFS-RT timestamps (POSIX seconds since 1970-01-01). The key is a `DATE` copy of the `TIMESTAMP`, because the 26ai Spatial documentation supports interval partitioning with `SPATIAL_INDEX_V2` only for a `NUMBER` or `DATE` key. Both tables use the same pattern. A table can have at most 1,048,575 partitions, so hourly partitions counted from 1970 last until about 2089. |
      | Global primary keys | The streaming `MERGE` checks each new position with one index probe instead of one probe per partition. |
      | `NOCOMPRESS` | The Data Lakehouse workload compresses tables with Hybrid Columnar Compression by default. That suits bulk loads, not a continuous stream of small inserts. Closed hours can be compressed later. |
      | `NOCACHE` | Large tables read with full scans shouldn't push frequently used blocks out of the buffer cache. This is the default; it's stated explicitly here. |
      | `ID` identity column in each table: `INTEGER GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY (START WITH 1 INCREMENT BY 1 CACHE 1000 MAXVALUE 9999999999)` | A surrogate key generated by the database. In `ZET_FEED_RAW` it is the primary key, and `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS.FEED_ID` refers to it. In `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS` the primary key stays `(vehicle_id, observed_at)`, because the `MERGE` needs it; `ID` gets no index, so it adds no index maintenance to the stream. The 26ai SQL reference recommends an identity cache above the default of 20, and the database skills recommend 1000 or more for high-volume inserts. `MAXVALUE 9999999999` allows 10 billion rows, about 16 years at 1.7 million positions a day. |

      **Left out on purpose:**
      * **`LOGGING` / `NOLOGGING`:** Autonomous AI Database ignores the logging clause; every change is logged for backup and recovery. On other databases, `NOLOGGING` only speeds up direct-path loads and leaves the data unrecoverable until the next backup.
      * **LOB `CACHE` / `NOCACHE`:** the 26ai JSON guide recommends `CACHE` for JSON stored in LOBs. Autonomous AI Database ignores LOB storage parameters other than compression, and `payload` uses the native JSON type.
      * **`PCTFREE`, `INITRANS`:** Autonomous AI Database ignores physical properties.
      * **Fast ingest (`MEMOPTIMIZE FOR WRITE`):** not possible for `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS`, because fast ingest doesn't allow a domain index such as the CBTREE spatial index (ORA-62191).

      **Retention.** `ZET_STREAM.PURGE` (section 6) deletes rows, which also works on partitioned tables. Dropping whole partitions is much faster for large volumes. For `ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS`, that needs a `LOCAL` spatial index, and whether a CBTREE index can be created `LOCAL` is still to be verified.

      References:
      * [Autonomous AI Database: SQL commands (ignored clauses)](https://docs.oracle.com/en-us/iaas/autonomous-database-serverless/doc/autonomous-sql-commands.html)
      * [Lakehouse workload (default compression)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/paas/autonomous-database/serverless/adbsb/appendix-autonomous-database-data-warehouse-workload.html)
      * [Creating a spatial index (26ai: partitioning, CBTREE)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/spatl/creating-spatial-index.html)
      * [CREATE TABLE (26ai: identity CACHE)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/sqlrf/CREATE-TABLE.html)
      * [LOB storage for JSON data (26ai)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/adjsn/considerations-when-using-lob-storage-json-data.html)
      * [ORA-62191](https://docs.oracle.com/en/error-help/db/ora-62191)
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Landing table: one row per feed message (GTFS-RT FeedMessage as JSON)

      -- Streaming design: hourly interval partitions, uncompressed, NOCACHE,
      cached identity

      --


      CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS zet_feed_raw (
        id           INTEGER
                     GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY (START WITH 1 INCREMENT BY 1 CACHE 1000 MAXVALUE 9999999999)
                     CONSTRAINT zet_feed_raw_pk PRIMARY KEY
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'Surrogate key, one row per received feed message'),
        feed_ts      TIMESTAMP
                     NOT NULL
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'GTFS-RT header timestamp (UTC)'),
        received_at  TIMESTAMP
                     DEFAULT ON NULL SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP)
                     NOT NULL
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'Time the message reached the database (UTC)'),
        received_date DATE
                     GENERATED ALWAYS AS (CAST(received_at AS DATE)) VIRTUAL,  -- partitioning key (UTC)
        payload      JSON
                     NOT NULL
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'GTFS-RT FeedMessage in the Protocol Buffers JSON mapping')
      )

      NOCOMPRESS

      PARTITION BY RANGE (received_date) INTERVAL (NUMTODSINTERVAL(1, 'HOUR'))

      (
        PARTITION p_epoch VALUES LESS THAN (DATE '1970-01-01')   -- transition point 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC; hourly partitions follow
      )

      NOCACHE

      ANNOTATIONS (
        Display 'ZET feed messages',
        Description 'Raw ZET GTFS-Realtime feed messages, kept for replay and reprocessing, one partition per UTC hour'
      );
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Vehicle positions: one row per vehicle and position timestamp

      -- Streaming design: hourly interval partitions, global primary key,
      uncompressed, NOCACHE

      --


      CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS zet_vehicle_positions (
        id           INTEGER
                     GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY (START WITH 1 INCREMENT BY 1 CACHE 1000 MAXVALUE 9999999999)
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'Surrogate key, one row per stored position'),
        vehicle_id   VARCHAR2(20)
                     NOT NULL
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'ZET vehicle id (GTFS-RT vehicle.vehicle.id)'),
        observed_at  TIMESTAMP
                     NOT NULL
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'Position timestamp in UTC (GTFS-RT vehicle.timestamp)'),
        observed_date DATE
                     GENERATED ALWAYS AS (CAST(observed_at AS DATE)) VIRTUAL,  -- partitioning key (UTC)
        route_id     VARCHAR2(10)
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'ZET line number, for example 6 for tram 6'),
        trip_id      VARCHAR2(64)
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'GTFS trip id, joins to the ZET GTFS schedule'),
        start_date   DATE
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'Service date of the trip'),
        feed_id      NUMBER
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'Feed message the position came from (ZET_FEED_RAW.ID)'),
        geom         SDO_GEOMETRY
                     NOT NULL
                     ANNOTATIONS (Description 'Vehicle position, WGS84 lon/lat (SRID 4326)'),
        CONSTRAINT zet_vehicle_positions_pk PRIMARY KEY (vehicle_id, observed_at)    -- global index: one probe per MERGE row
      )

      NOCOMPRESS

      PARTITION BY RANGE (observed_date) INTERVAL (NUMTODSINTERVAL(1, 'HOUR'))

      (
        PARTITION p_epoch VALUES LESS THAN (DATE '1970-01-01')   -- transition point 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC; hourly partitions follow
      )

      NOCACHE

      ANNOTATIONS (
        Display 'ZET vehicle positions',
        Description 'Positions of ZET trams and buses from the GTFS-Realtime feed, Zagreb, one partition per UTC hour'
      );
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      ## 6. Ingest Procedure


      `ZET_STREAM.INGEST` takes one feed message as JSON text (the loader or the
      ORDS endpoint passes it through unchanged) and runs two steps:

      1. It stores the message in `ZET_FEED_RAW`.

      2. It extracts the vehicle positions with `JSON_TABLE`, building each
      point with the 26ai constructor `SDO_GEOMETRY(lon, lat)`, and merges them
      in one statement.


      Trip update entities have no `vehicle.position`; the JSON path filter
      skips them.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      CREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE zet_stream AS

        -- Ingest one GTFS-RT feed message (JSON). Returns the feed id and the number of new positions.
        PROCEDURE ingest (
          p_payload IN  CLOB,
          p_feed_id OUT NUMBER,
          p_rows    OUT NUMBER
        );

        -- Delete positions and raw messages older than p_keep_hours
        PROCEDURE purge (
          p_keep_hours IN NUMBER DEFAULT 24
        );

      END zet_stream;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      CREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY zet_stream AS

        PROCEDURE ingest (
          p_payload IN  CLOB,
          p_feed_id OUT NUMBER,
          p_rows    OUT NUMBER
        ) IS
        BEGIN
          -- 1. Land the raw feed message
          INSERT INTO zet_feed_raw (feed_ts, payload)
          VALUES (
            TIMESTAMP '1970-01-01 00:00:00'
              + NUMTODSINTERVAL(TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(p_payload, '$.header.timestamp')), 'SECOND'),
            JSON(p_payload)
          )
          RETURNING id INTO p_feed_id;

          -- 2. Extract the vehicle positions set-based and skip positions that are already stored
          MERGE INTO zet_vehicle_positions t
          USING (
            SELECT
              j.vehicle_id,
              TIMESTAMP '1970-01-01 00:00:00' + NUMTODSINTERVAL(TO_NUMBER(j.epoch), 'SECOND') AS observed_at,
              j.route_id,
              j.trip_id,
              TO_DATE(j.start_date, 'YYYYMMDD') AS start_date,
              SDO_GEOMETRY(j.lon, j.lat) AS geom                     -- 26ai lon/lat constructor (2001, 4326)
            FROM
              zet_feed_raw r,
              JSON_TABLE(
                r.payload, '$.entity[*]?(exists(@.vehicle.position))'
                COLUMNS (
                  vehicle_id VARCHAR2(20) PATH '$.vehicle.vehicle.id',
                  epoch      VARCHAR2(20) PATH '$.vehicle.timestamp',
                  route_id   VARCHAR2(10) PATH '$.vehicle.trip.routeId',
                  trip_id    VARCHAR2(64) PATH '$.vehicle.trip.tripId',
                  start_date VARCHAR2(8)  PATH '$.vehicle.trip.startDate',
                  lat        NUMBER       PATH '$.vehicle.position.latitude',
                  lon        NUMBER       PATH '$.vehicle.position.longitude'
                )
              ) j
            WHERE
              r.id = p_feed_id
              AND j.vehicle_id IS NOT NULL
              AND j.epoch IS NOT NULL
              AND j.lat IS NOT NULL
              AND j.lon IS NOT NULL
              AND NOT (j.lat = 0 AND j.lon = 0)
          ) s
          ON (t.vehicle_id = s.vehicle_id AND t.observed_at = s.observed_at)
          WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
            INSERT (vehicle_id, observed_at, route_id, trip_id, start_date, feed_id, geom)
            VALUES (s.vehicle_id, s.observed_at, s.route_id, s.trip_id, s.start_date, p_feed_id, s.geom);

          p_rows := SQL%ROWCOUNT;
        END ingest;

        PROCEDURE purge (
          p_keep_hours IN NUMBER DEFAULT 24
        ) IS
          l_limit TIMESTAMP := SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP) - NUMTODSINTERVAL(p_keep_hours, 'HOUR');
        BEGIN
          DELETE FROM zet_vehicle_positions WHERE observed_at < l_limit;
          DELETE FROM zet_feed_raw WHERE received_at < l_limit;
        END purge;

      END zet_stream;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      ## 7. Test with Real and Reconstructed Feed Data


      Section 7 loads two data sets, both before the index is created in section
      8:

      * **7.1 One real feed message** tests the ingest path end to end.

      * **7.2 Three hot days in July 2026** adds about 1.1 million tram
      positions for the analysis and the map.


      ### 7.1 One Real Feed Message


      The sample below holds the **13 tram 6 vehicles** from the ZET feed of
      2026-10-08, 11:47 UTC, plus one trip update entity that the ingest has to
      skip. It uses the same JSON form the loader sends.


      Run the cell twice. The second run reports **0 new positions**, which
      shows that the ingest is idempotent.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Ingest one real feed sample (tram 6, 2026-10-08 11:47 UTC)

      --


      DECLARE
        l_feed_id NUMBER;
        l_rows    NUMBER;
      BEGIN
        zet_stream.ingest(
          p_payload => q'~
      {
        "header":{"gtfsRealtimeVersion":"1.0","incrementality":"FULL_DATASET","timestamp":"1791460060"},
        "entity":[
          {"id":"XQE3KMT01E","tripUpdate":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_1116_11_11542","routeId":"11"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT02M_102205","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_608_6_11873","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.792652,"longitude":16.001947},"timestamp":"1791460059","vehicle":{"id":"102205"}}},
          {"id":"XNE3KMT0CT_102263","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_601_6_11876","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.809143,"longitude":15.977969},"timestamp":"1791460055","vehicle":{"id":"102263"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT00V_102255","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_605_6_11960","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.777744,"longitude":15.98805},"timestamp":"1791460059","vehicle":{"id":"102255"}}},
          {"id":"XNE3KMT0E2_102215","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_603_6_11926","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.802128,"longitude":15.994857},"timestamp":"1791460060","vehicle":{"id":"102215"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT032_102225","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_609_6_11901","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.803661,"longitude":15.993952},"timestamp":"1791460048","vehicle":{"id":"102225"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT006_102289","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_604_6_11992","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.82214,"longitude":16.036694},"timestamp":"1791460051","vehicle":{"id":"102289"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT07C_102221","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_616_6_11850","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.813095,"longitude":15.96},"timestamp":"1791460058","vehicle":{"id":"102221"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT01F_10477","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_606_6_11988","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.777645,"longitude":15.983942},"timestamp":"1791460059","vehicle":{"id":"10477"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT047_22135","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_611_6_11967","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.820286,"longitude":16.038536},"timestamp":"1791460062","vehicle":{"id":"22135"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT03N_102237","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_610_6_11923","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.805962,"longitude":15.978015},"timestamp":"1791460054","vehicle":{"id":"102237"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT04W_102279","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_612_6_11949","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.812248,"longitude":15.963031},"timestamp":"1791460059","vehicle":{"id":"102279"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT065_22133","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_614_6_12009","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.815006,"longitude":15.933783},"timestamp":"1791460053","vehicle":{"id":"22133"}}},
          {"id":"XOE3KMT05G_10416","vehicle":{"trip":{"tripId":"0_30_613_6_11983","startDate":"20261008","routeId":"6"},"position":{"latitude":45.813698,"longitude":15.941017},"timestamp":"1791460059","vehicle":{"id":"10416"}}}
        ]
      }

      ~',
          p_feed_id => l_feed_id,
          p_rows    => l_rows
        );
        COMMIT;
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('feed_id=' || l_feed_id || ', new positions=' || l_rows);
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Show the positions, with local Zagreb time

      --


      SELECT
        p.vehicle_id,
        p.route_id,
        p.observed_at AS observed_at_utc,
        CAST(FROM_TZ(p.observed_at, 'UTC') AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP) AS observed_at_local,
        p.geom.sdo_point.x AS lon,
        p.geom.sdo_point.y AS lat
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions p
      WHERE
        p.geom.sdo_gtype = SDO_POINT2D
        AND p.geom.sdo_srid = SDO_LONLAT
      ORDER BY
        p.observed_at;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Sanity check: distance of each tram 6 position to the tram 6 track
      (OSM)

      -- Vehicles far from the track are depot runs or diversions.

      --


      SELECT
        p.vehicle_id,
        ROUND(MIN(SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE(p.geom, l.geom, 0.05, 'unit=M'))) AS distance_to_track_m
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions p,
        tram_lines l
      WHERE
        p.route_id = l.line_ref
      GROUP BY
        p.vehicle_id
      ORDER BY
        distance_to_track_m;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ### 7.2 Three Hot Days: 29–31 July 2026

      **Why these days.**
      * DHMZ dates the longest and most widespread heat wave of summer 2026 from **28 July to 16 August**.
      * On **30 July** the Zagreb region was under an orange heat warning.
      * From **31 July to 6 August** the heat-wave criteria were met at all eight main stations, Zagreb-Maksimir included.
      * All three days are weekdays with full tram service.

      Sources:
      * [DHMZ heat-wave analysis 2026 (morski.hr)](https://www.morski.hr/dhmz-analizirao-toplinske-valove-u-2026-cak-6-od-8-gradova-srusilo-povijesne-rekorde-evo-gdje-je-bilo-najgore/)
      * [Toplinski val u Hrvatskoj 2026. (Wikipedia)](https://hr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toplinski_val_u_Hrvatskoj_2026.)
      * [tportal, 30 July 2026](https://www.tportal.hr/vijesti/clanak/cijela-hrvatska-pod-meteoalarmom-neki-gradovi-gore-od-sest-ujutro-20260730)

      **Reconstructed, not recorded.** There are no GTFS-Realtime recordings of ZET for July 2026: the public Zagreb recordings in [GISBoost/easy-GTFS-RT](https://github.com/GISBoost/easy-GTFS-RT/releases) start on 2026-09-17. So `scripts/gen_zet_july_positions.py` reconstructs the positions from the official [ZET GTFS timetable](https://zet.hr/gtfs-scheduled/latest):
      * Every weekday tram trip (service `0_30`, 3,838 trips on 19 tram lines) moves along its GTFS shape according to its timetable and dwells at its stops.
      * Each block (187 vehicle duties) reports a position about every 30 seconds, including short layovers at the terminus.
      * Small random delays per trip and about 2.5 m of position noise make it look like a live feed.
      * Vehicle ids are `SIM-<block_id>` and `feed_id` stays NULL, so reconstructed rows never mix with real feed data.

      **Caveat.** The published GTFS (feed version 000396) is valid from 2026-09-28. Its weekday timetable is applied to the July dates. ZET's July summer timetable ran fewer trips, so the frequencies are approximate.

      | File in `data/` | Positions | Size |
      |---|---|---|
      | `zet_tram_positions_20260729.csv.gz` | 365,929 | 4.8 MB |
      | `zet_tram_positions_20260730.csv.gz` | 366,125 | 4.8 MB |
      | `zet_tram_positions_20260731.csv.gz` | 366,074 | 4.8 MB |

      Columns: `VEHICLE_ID, OBSERVED_AT_UTC (YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS), ROUTE_ID, TRIP_ID, START_DATE (YYYYMMDD), LON, LAT`.

      Checks run while generating the files:
      * Tram 6 positions lie a median of 1.5 m from the OSM tram 6 track.
      * Between 14:00 and 16:00 Zagreb time on 29 July, 95 trams on lines 1, 6, 11, 12, 13, 14 and 17 pass within 150 m of stop A.

      **Loading.** Upload the files to OCI Object Storage, read them through an external table, and insert them with one set-based `INSERT ... SELECT`:

      ```bash
      # Upload the three files and create a read-only pre-authenticated request (PAR) for the bucket
      oci os object bulk-upload --bucket-name <bucket> --src-dir ../data --include "zet_tram_positions_202607*.csv.gz"
      oci os preauth-request create --bucket-name <bucket> --name zet-july-2026 --access-type AnyObjectRead --time-expires 2026-12-31T00:00:00Z
      ```

      **Without Object Storage:**
      1. Unzip the files.
      2. Create a staging table with the same columns as the external table.
      3. Load each file with SQLcl, for example `LOAD zet_tram_positions_july_stage ../data/zet_tram_positions_20260729.csv BATCHSIZE 5000`.
      4. Run the `INSERT` below against the staging table.

      **Querying the July data in sections 9 and 10.** The section 9 queries use the July time range with fixed UTC windows. For example, 14:00–16:00 Zagreb time on 29 July is `p.observed_at BETWEEN TIMESTAMP '2026-07-29 12:00:00' AND TIMESTAMP '2026-07-29 14:00:00'`. The live vehicle tiles in section 10 still use a window relative to now, so they show streamed positions, not the July data.

      **Keep the July data.** Section 12 creates an hourly purge job that keeps only the last 24 hours, so it would delete the July data at its next run. While you work with the July data, skip that cell, or create the job with a larger `p_keep_hours`.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- External table over the three files in Object Storage

      -- Paste the PAR URL (ending in /o/) into l_par

      --


      DECLARE
        --l_par VARCHAR2(1000) := '<par-url-ending-in-/o/>';
        l_par VARCHAR2(1000) := 'https://objectstorage.eu-frankfurt-1.oraclecloud.com/p/DSXeoE4yVGWojcH5Wk17fCm_TH7yydmn93FdTYYcUZyrfUjDRCWDdamEPKAgwy6E/n/oradbclouducm/b/hroug2026-bucket/o/';   -- paste your pre-authenticated request URL here; never commit it
      BEGIN
        DBMS_CLOUD.CREATE_EXTERNAL_TABLE(
          table_name      => 'ZET_TRAM_POSITIONS_JULY_EXT',
          credential_name => NULL,                                   -- a PAR URL needs no credential
          file_uri_list   => l_par || 'zet_tram_positions_20260729.csv.gz,'
                          || l_par || 'zet_tram_positions_20260730.csv.gz,'
                          || l_par || 'zet_tram_positions_20260731.csv.gz',
          column_list     => 'vehicle_id      VARCHAR2(20),
                              observed_at_utc VARCHAR2(19),
                              route_id        VARCHAR2(10),
                              trip_id         VARCHAR2(64),
                              start_date      VARCHAR2(8),
                              lon             NUMBER,
                              lat             NUMBER',
          format          => JSON_OBJECT('type' VALUE 'csv', 'skipheaders' VALUE 1, 'compression' VALUE 'gzip')
        );
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Check the files before loading: expected 365,929 / 366,125 / 366,074
      positions and 187 trams per day

      --


      SELECT
        start_date,
        COUNT(*) AS positions,
        COUNT(DISTINCT vehicle_id) AS trams,
        MIN(observed_at_utc) AS first_utc,
        MAX(observed_at_utc) AS last_utc
      FROM
        zet_tram_positions_july_ext
      GROUP BY
        start_date
      ORDER BY
        start_date;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Bulk load: one direct-path INSERT ... SELECT, points built with
      SDO_GEOMETRY(lon, lat)

      -- (APPEND falls back to a conventional insert where direct path does not
      apply)

      --


      INSERT /*+ APPEND */ INTO zet_vehicle_positions (
        vehicle_id,
        observed_at,
        route_id,
        trip_id,
        start_date,
        feed_id,
        geom
      )

      SELECT
        vehicle_id,
        TO_TIMESTAMP(observed_at_utc, 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS'),
        route_id,
        trip_id,
        TO_DATE(start_date, 'YYYYMMDD'),
        NULL,                                                        -- no feed message: reconstructed from the timetable
        SDO_GEOMETRY(lon, lat)                                       -- 26ai lon/lat constructor (2001, 4326)
      FROM
        zet_tram_positions_july_ext;

      COMMIT;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "[Tram
      positions](https://ij1tyzir3wpwlpe-kpadw23ai.adb.eu-frankfurt-1.oracleclo\
      udapps.com/spatialstudio/api/v1/jsonstream/51825a461c3810ad7a828ca424afac\
      ca) are available as GeoJSON via Spatial Studio."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Gather table statistics after the bulk load (1.1 million new rows), so
      the optimizer

      -- plans the following queries with current row counts. Default
      granularity: global and partition statistics.

      --


      BEGIN
        DBMS_STATS.GATHER_TABLE_STATS(
          ownname          => USER,
          tabname          => 'ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS',
          estimate_percent => DBMS_STATS.AUTO_SAMPLE_SIZE,
          degree           => DBMS_STATS.AUTO_DEGREE
        );
      END;

      /


      --

      -- Check: rows in the table statistics and the hourly partitions that
      received rows

      -- (75 for the July data, plus 1 for the 8 October sample from 7.1)

      --


      SELECT
        table_name,
        num_rows,
        last_analyzed
      FROM
        user_tables
      WHERE
        table_name = 'ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS';

      SELECT
        COUNT(*) AS partitions_with_rows,
        SUM(num_rows) AS rows_in_partitions
      FROM
        user_tab_partitions
      WHERE
        table_name = 'ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS'
        AND num_rows > 0;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- The three hot days per service date (Zagreb local time)

      --


      SELECT
        TO_CHAR(start_date, 'YYYY-MM-DD') AS service_date,
        COUNT(*) AS positions,
        COUNT(DISTINCT vehicle_id) AS trams,
        COUNT(DISTINCT route_id) AS tram_lines,
        MIN(CAST(FROM_TZ(observed_at, 'UTC') AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP)) AS first_local,
        MAX(CAST(FROM_TZ(observed_at, 'UTC') AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP)) AS last_local
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions
      WHERE
        vehicle_id LIKE 'SIM-%'
      GROUP BY
        start_date
      ORDER BY
        start_date;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Trams in service per hour, 29 July 2026 (Zagreb local time)

      --


      SELECT
        local_hour,
        COUNT(DISTINCT vehicle_id) AS active_trams,
        COUNT(*) AS positions
      FROM (
        SELECT
          vehicle_id,
          EXTRACT(HOUR FROM CAST(FROM_TZ(observed_at, 'UTC') AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP)) AS local_hour
        FROM
          zet_vehicle_positions
        WHERE
          vehicle_id LIKE 'SIM-%'
          AND start_date = DATE '2026-07-29'
      )

      GROUP BY
        local_hour
      ORDER BY
        local_hour;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- The data now lives in ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS: the external table is no
      longer needed

      --


      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS zet_tram_positions_july_ext;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      ## 8. Spatio-temporal CBTREE Index


      Create the index **after** the first positions are in. 26ai then creates
      the spatial metadata automatically.


      `FILTER BY observed_at` adds the timestamp to the CBTREE index, so a query
      that filters by place and time is evaluated in a single index search.


      `SDO_POINT_FIELD_ONLY=TRUE` is recommended because all points are stored
      in the `SDO_POINT` attribute.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Create the spatio-temporal index (geometry + timestamp)

      --


      CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS zet_vehicle_positions_stidx ON
      zet_vehicle_positions (geom)
        INDEXTYPE IS MDSYS.SPATIAL_INDEX_V2
        FILTER BY observed_at
        PARAMETERS ('cbtree_index=true layer_gtype=point sdo_point_field_only=true');
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Check: automatically created spatial metadata and the index

      --


      SELECT * FROM user_sdo_geom_metadata WHERE table_name =
      'ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS';


      SELECT index_name, table_name, column_name, sdo_index_type FROM
      user_sdo_index_info

      WHERE table_name = 'ZET_VEHICLE_POSITIONS';
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      ## 9. Spatio-temporal Queries


      **Which vehicles passed stop A (Trg bana Josipa Jelačića) within the time
      window?**


      The query window comes from a join with `TRAM_STATIONS`, so keep the
      `DOMAIN_INDEX_FILTER` hint, as in the CBTREE template notebook.


      The queries cover the analyzed July time range: the three hot days 29–31
      July 2026 in Zagreb time, loaded in section 7.2. Zagreb is at UTC+2 in
      summer, so in UTC the range runs from 28 July 22:00:00 to 31 July
      21:59:59. The real feed sample from section 7.1 (8 October) lies outside
      this range. Once the loader is streaming, use a window relative to now,
      for example the last 30 minutes: `p.observed_at >=
      SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP) - INTERVAL '30' MINUTE`.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Explain plan: spatial and temporal predicates evaluated in the CBTREE
      index

      --


      EXPLAIN PLAN FOR

      SELECT
        /*+ NO_RESULT_CACHE DOMAIN_INDEX_FILTER(p zet_vehicle_positions_stidx) */
        p.vehicle_id,
        p.route_id,
        COUNT(*) AS positions
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions p,
        tram_stations s
      WHERE
        s.station_code = 'A'
        AND SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE(p.geom, s.geom, 'distance=150 unit=M') = 'TRUE'
        AND p.observed_at BETWEEN
          TIMESTAMP '2026-07-28 22:00:00'        -- 29 July 2026, 00:00:00 Zagreb time (UTC+2)
          AND TIMESTAMP '2026-07-31 21:59:59'    -- 31 July 2026, 23:59:59 Zagreb time
      GROUP BY
        p.vehicle_id,
        p.route_id;

      SELECT * FROM TABLE(DBMS_XPLAN.DISPLAY);
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Vehicles within 150 m of stop A on the hot days, 29–31 July 2026
      (Zagreb time)

      --


      SELECT
        /*+ NO_RESULT_CACHE DOMAIN_INDEX_FILTER(p zet_vehicle_positions_stidx) */
        p.vehicle_id,
        p.route_id,
        MIN(CAST(FROM_TZ(p.observed_at, 'UTC') AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP)) AS first_seen_local,
        MAX(CAST(FROM_TZ(p.observed_at, 'UTC') AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP)) AS last_seen_local,
        COUNT(*) AS positions
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions p,
        tram_stations s
      WHERE
        s.station_code = 'A'
        AND SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE(p.geom, s.geom, 'distance=150 unit=M') = 'TRUE'
        AND p.observed_at BETWEEN
          TIMESTAMP '2026-07-28 22:00:00'        -- 29 July 2026, 00:00:00 Zagreb time (UTC+2)
          AND TIMESTAMP '2026-07-31 21:59:59'    -- 31 July 2026, 23:59:59 Zagreb time
      GROUP BY
        p.vehicle_id,
        p.route_id
      ORDER BY
        first_seen_local;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "**The important question:** which trams passed stop A between 14:00 and
      16:00 Zagreb time on each of the three hot days, 29–31 July 2026? Each
      local time window is converted to UTC once, in the `WITH` clause. The
      three days come from a table value constructor (`VALUES`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Trams at stop A between 14:00 and 16:00 local time (Europe/Zagreb),

      -- on each hot day, 29–31 July 2026

      --


      WITH win AS (
        SELECT
          d.local_day,
          SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(FROM_TZ(CAST(d.local_day AS TIMESTAMP) + INTERVAL '14' HOUR, 'Europe/Zagreb')) AS t_from,
          SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(FROM_TZ(CAST(d.local_day AS TIMESTAMP) + INTERVAL '16' HOUR, 'Europe/Zagreb')) AS t_to
        FROM
          (VALUES (DATE '2026-07-29'), (DATE '2026-07-30'), (DATE '2026-07-31')) d (local_day)
      )


      SELECT
        /*+ NO_RESULT_CACHE DOMAIN_INDEX_FILTER(p zet_vehicle_positions_stidx) */
        win.local_day,
        p.route_id,
        COUNT(DISTINCT p.vehicle_id) AS vehicles,
        COUNT(*) AS positions
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions p,
        tram_stations s,
        win
      WHERE
        s.station_code = 'A'
        AND SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE(p.geom, s.geom, 'distance=150 unit=M') = 'TRUE'
        AND p.observed_at BETWEEN win.t_from AND win.t_to
      GROUP BY
        win.local_day,
        p.route_id
      ORDER BY
        win.local_day,
        vehicles DESC;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ## 10. Vector Tiles

      `SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE` returns a Mapbox Vector Tile (BLOB) for a tile address `z/x/y` in the Google tiling scheme.
      * **Static layers:** `TRAM_LINES` and `TRAM_STATIONS` use the table form of the function.
      * **Live layer:** the vehicles use the cursor form, so the tile query can filter by time as well (last 2 minutes by default).

      References:
      * [SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/23/spatl/sdo_util-get_vectortile.html)
      * [SDO_UTIL.GET_TILE_ENVELOPE](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/spatl/sdo_util-get_tile_envelope.html)

      Tile addresses for stop A: zoom 12 → 2229/1460, 13 → 4459/2920, 14 → 8919/5841, 15 → 17838/11682.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Tile coordinates (Google tiling scheme) for stop A at zoom levels 12 to
      15

      --


      SELECT
        z.zoom,
        FLOOR((s.geom.sdo_point.x + 180) / 360 * POWER(2, z.zoom)) AS tile_x,
        FLOOR(
          (1 - LN(TAN(s.geom.sdo_point.y * ACOS(-1) / 180) + 1 / COS(s.geom.sdo_point.y * ACOS(-1) / 180)) / ACOS(-1))
          / 2 * POWER(2, z.zoom)
        ) AS tile_y
      FROM
        tram_stations s,
        (SELECT LEVEL + 11 AS zoom FROM dual CONNECT BY LEVEL <= 4) z
      WHERE
        s.station_code = 'A'
      ORDER BY
        z.zoom;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Tile envelope of tile 14/8919/5841 (city centre with stop A)

      --


      SELECT SDO_UTIL.GET_TILE_ENVELOPE(tile_zoom => 14, tile_x => 8919, tile_y
      => 5841) AS tile_envelope FROM dual;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Static layers: vector tiles for the tram 6 track and stops (size in
      bytes)

      --


      SELECT
        DBMS_LOB.GETLENGTH(
          SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE(
            TABLE_NAME    => 'TRAM_LINES',
            GEOM_COL_NAME => 'GEOM',
            ATT_COL_NAMES => SDO_STRING_ARRAY('LINE_REF', 'ROUTE_NAME'),
            TILE_X        => 8919,
            TILE_Y        => 5841,
            TILE_ZOOM     => 14,
            LAYER_NAME    => 'tram_lines'
          )
        ) AS tram_lines_tile_bytes,
        DBMS_LOB.GETLENGTH(
          SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE(
            TABLE_NAME    => 'TRAM_STATIONS',
            GEOM_COL_NAME => 'GEOM',
            ATT_COL_NAMES => SDO_STRING_ARRAY('STATION_CODE', 'STATION_NAME'),
            TILE_X        => 8919,
            TILE_Y        => 5841,
            TILE_ZOOM     => 14,
            LAYER_NAME    => 'tram_stations'
          )
        ) AS tram_stations_tile_bytes
      FROM
        dual;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      **Live layer.** `ZET_TILES.VEHICLES` builds the tile from a cursor:

      * It computes the tile envelope once, so the query has no join and the
      optimizer can use the CBTREE time filter from statistics. This matters
      because ORDS sessions on the Data Lakehouse workload ignore hints.

      * It returns the positions of the last `p_window_s` seconds with their
      age, so the map can fade older positions into a short trail.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      CREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE zet_tiles AS

        -- Vector tile (layer "vehicles") with the positions of the last p_window_s seconds
        FUNCTION vehicles (
          p_z        IN NUMBER,
          p_x        IN NUMBER,
          p_y        IN NUMBER,
          p_window_s IN NUMBER DEFAULT 120
        ) RETURN BLOB;

      END zet_tiles;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      CREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY zet_tiles AS

        FUNCTION vehicles (
          p_z        IN NUMBER,
          p_x        IN NUMBER,
          p_y        IN NUMBER,
          p_window_s IN NUMBER DEFAULT 120
        ) RETURN BLOB IS
          l_now      TIMESTAMP     := SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP);
          l_from     TIMESTAMP     := l_now - NUMTODSINTERVAL(p_window_s, 'SECOND');
          l_envelope SDO_GEOMETRY  := SDO_UTIL.GET_TILE_ENVELOPE(tile_zoom => p_z, tile_x => p_x, tile_y => p_y);
          l_params   VARCHAR2(200) := 'mask=anyinteract min_resolution='
                                      || TO_CHAR((40075016.0 / POWER(2, p_z)) * .001, 'FM999999990.0000', q'[NLS_NUMERIC_CHARACTERS='.,']')
                                      || ' bypass_point=true';
          l_cur      SYS_REFCURSOR;
        BEGIN
          OPEN l_cur FOR
            SELECT
              /*+ DOMAIN_INDEX_FILTER(v zet_vehicle_positions_stidx) */
              v.geom,                                                            -- geometry first
              v.vehicle_id,
              v.route_id,
              ROUND((CAST(l_now AS DATE) - CAST(v.observed_at AS DATE)) * 86400) AS age_s
            FROM
              zet_vehicle_positions v
            WHERE
              v.observed_at >= l_from
              AND SDO_RELATE(v.geom, l_envelope, l_params) = 'TRUE';

          RETURN SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE(
            cur        => l_cur,
            tile_x     => p_x,
            tile_y     => p_y,
            tile_zoom  => p_z,
            layer_name => 'vehicles'
          );
        END vehicles;

      END zet_tiles;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Live layer test: tile 14/8919/5841 with a 24 hour window (finds the
      sample from section 7)

      --


      SELECT DBMS_LOB.GETLENGTH(zet_tiles.vehicles(14, 8919, 5841, 86400)) AS
      vehicles_tile_bytes FROM dual;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      ## 11. REST Endpoints with ORDS


      | Module | Endpoint | Method | Access |

      |---|---|---|---|

      | `zet.tiles` | `/ords/<schema>/zet/vt/vehicles/{z}/{x}/{y}` | GET |
      Public, read-only |

      | `zet.tiles` | `/ords/<schema>/zet/vt/tram-lines/{z}/{x}/{y}` | GET |
      Public, read-only |

      | `zet.tiles` | `/ords/<schema>/zet/vt/tram-stations/{z}/{x}/{y}` | GET |
      Public, read-only |

      | `zet.ingest` | `/ords/<schema>/zet-ingest/positions` | POST | OAuth
      client credentials (privilege `zet.ingest.write`) |


      The tile handlers use the `media` source type: the first column is the
      content type and the second is the tile BLOB.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: |
      --
      -- REST-enable the schema (no-op if already enabled)
      --

      BEGIN
        ORDS.ENABLE_SCHEMA(
          p_enabled             => TRUE,
          p_url_mapping_type    => 'BASE_PATH',
          p_url_mapping_pattern => LOWER(USER),
          p_auto_rest_auth      => TRUE
        );
        COMMIT;
      END;
      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Public tile module: live vehicles and the static tram 6 layers

      --


      BEGIN
        ORDS.DEFINE_MODULE(
          p_module_name => 'zet.tiles',
          p_base_path   => '/zet/',
          p_status      => 'PUBLISHED',
          p_comments    => 'ZET vector tiles (Mapbox Vector Tile format)'
        );

        -- Live vehicle positions (last 2 minutes)
        ORDS.DEFINE_TEMPLATE(p_module_name => 'zet.tiles', p_pattern => 'vt/vehicles/:z/:x/:y');
        ORDS.DEFINE_HANDLER(
          p_module_name => 'zet.tiles',
          p_pattern     => 'vt/vehicles/:z/:x/:y',
          p_method      => 'GET',
          p_source_type => ORDS.source_type_media,
          p_source      => q'[
            SELECT 'application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile',
                   zet_tiles.vehicles(TO_NUMBER(:z), TO_NUMBER(:x), TO_NUMBER(:y))
            FROM dual]'
        );

        -- Tram 6 track
        ORDS.DEFINE_TEMPLATE(p_module_name => 'zet.tiles', p_pattern => 'vt/tram-lines/:z/:x/:y');
        ORDS.DEFINE_HANDLER(
          p_module_name => 'zet.tiles',
          p_pattern     => 'vt/tram-lines/:z/:x/:y',
          p_method      => 'GET',
          p_source_type => ORDS.source_type_media,
          p_source      => q'[
            SELECT 'application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile',
                   SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE(
                     TABLE_NAME    => 'TRAM_LINES',
                     GEOM_COL_NAME => 'GEOM',
                     ATT_COL_NAMES => SDO_STRING_ARRAY('LINE_REF', 'ROUTE_NAME'),
                     TILE_X        => TO_NUMBER(:x),
                     TILE_Y        => TO_NUMBER(:y),
                     TILE_ZOOM     => TO_NUMBER(:z),
                     LAYER_NAME    => 'tram_lines')
            FROM dual]'
        );

        -- Tram 6 stops A-D
        ORDS.DEFINE_TEMPLATE(p_module_name => 'zet.tiles', p_pattern => 'vt/tram-stations/:z/:x/:y');
        ORDS.DEFINE_HANDLER(
          p_module_name => 'zet.tiles',
          p_pattern     => 'vt/tram-stations/:z/:x/:y',
          p_method      => 'GET',
          p_source_type => ORDS.source_type_media,
          p_source      => q'[
            SELECT 'application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile',
                   SDO_UTIL.GET_VECTORTILE(
                     TABLE_NAME    => 'TRAM_STATIONS',
                     GEOM_COL_NAME => 'GEOM',
                     ATT_COL_NAMES => SDO_STRING_ARRAY('STATION_CODE', 'STATION_NAME'),
                     TILE_X        => TO_NUMBER(:x),
                     TILE_Y        => TO_NUMBER(:y),
                     TILE_ZOOM     => TO_NUMBER(:z),
                     LAYER_NAME    => 'tram_stations')
            FROM dual]'
        );

        COMMIT;
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Protected ingest module: the loader POSTs one feed message (JSON) per
      poll

      --


      BEGIN
        ORDS.DEFINE_MODULE(
          p_module_name => 'zet.ingest',
          p_base_path   => '/zet-ingest/',
          p_status      => 'PUBLISHED',
          p_comments    => 'Ingest ZET GTFS-Realtime feed messages (Protocol Buffers JSON mapping)'
        );

        ORDS.DEFINE_TEMPLATE(p_module_name => 'zet.ingest', p_pattern => 'positions');
        ORDS.DEFINE_HANDLER(
          p_module_name   => 'zet.ingest',
          p_pattern       => 'positions',
          p_method        => 'POST',
          p_source_type   => ORDS.source_type_plsql,
          p_mimes_allowed => 'application/json',
          p_source        => q'[
            DECLARE
              l_payload CLOB := :body_text;      -- dereference the body bind exactly once
              l_feed_id NUMBER;
              l_rows    NUMBER;
            BEGIN
              zet_stream.ingest(l_payload, l_feed_id, l_rows);
              COMMIT;
              :status_code := 201;
              HTP.P('{"feed_id":' || l_feed_id || ',"new_positions":' || l_rows || '}');
            END;]'
        );

        COMMIT;
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Protect the ingest module with a privilege

      --


      DECLARE
        l_roles    OWA.VC_ARR;
        l_patterns OWA.VC_ARR;
        l_modules  OWA.VC_ARR;
      BEGIN
        ORDS.DEFINE_PRIVILEGE(
          p_privilege_name => 'zet.ingest.write',
          p_roles          => l_roles,
          p_patterns       => l_patterns,
          p_modules        => l_modules,
          p_label          => 'ZET feed ingest',
          p_description    => 'POST ZET GTFS-Realtime feed messages to /zet-ingest/positions'
        );

        ORDS.SET_MODULE_PRIVILEGE(
          p_module_name    => 'zet.ingest',
          p_privilege_name => 'zet.ingest.write'
        );

        COMMIT;
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      **OAuth client for the loader.** The next cell prints a client id and a
      client secret.


      **Copy them into the local `.credentials` file. Never into a script, never
      into this notebook, and never commit them.** Replace the support e-mail
      placeholder before running the cell.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Register the OAuth client used by the loader (client credentials flow)

      --


      DECLARE
        l_client ORDS_TYPES.T_CLIENT_CREDENTIALS;
      BEGIN
        l_client := ORDS_SECURITY.REGISTER_CLIENT(
          p_name            => 'zet-gtfsrt-loader',
          p_grant_type      => 'client_credentials',
          p_description     => 'Loader that posts ZET GTFS-Realtime feed messages',
          p_support_email   => 'karin.patenge@oracle.com',
          p_privilege_names => 'zet.ingest.write'
        );

        l_client.client_key.name := 'zet-gtfsrt-loader';
        l_client := ORDS_SECURITY.ROTATE_CLIENT_SECRET(
          p_client_key      => l_client.client_key,
          p_revoke_existing => TRUE
        );

        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('client_id=' || l_client.client_key.client_id);
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('client_secret=' || l_client.client_secret.secret);
        COMMIT;
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: |
      --
      -- Check the REST modules and templates
      --

      SELECT m.name AS module_name, m.uri_prefix, t.uri_template
      FROM user_ords_modules m JOIN user_ords_templates t ON t.module_id = m.id
      WHERE m.name LIKE 'zet.%'
      ORDER BY m.name, t.uri_template;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: >-
      **Test from the command line** (replace `<adb-host>` and `<schema>`):


      ```bash

      # Static tile, tram 6 track

      curl -s -o tram6.pbf
      https://<adb-host>/ords/<schema>/zet/vt/tram-lines/14/8919/5841


      # Token for the loader (client id and secret from .credentials)

      curl -s -u "$ZET_CLIENT_ID:$ZET_CLIENT_SECRET" -d
      "grant_type=client_credentials"
      https://<adb-host>/ords/<schema>/oauth/token


      # Post one feed message as JSON

      curl -s -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type:
      application/json" \
           --data-binary @feed.json https://<adb-host>/ords/<schema>/zet-ingest/positions
      ```


      The map adds the three tile URLs as `vector` sources (MapLibre GL JS). The
      live layer is refreshed every few seconds.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ## 12. Retention

      For the demo, a scheduler job keeps 24 hours of data.
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Hourly purge job: keep 24 hours of positions and raw messages

      --


      BEGIN
        DBMS_SCHEDULER.CREATE_JOB(
          job_name        => 'ZET_PURGE_JOB',
          job_type        => 'PLSQL_BLOCK',
          job_action      => 'BEGIN zet_stream.purge(p_keep_hours => 24); COMMIT; END;',
          start_date      => SYSTIMESTAMP,
          repeat_interval => 'FREQ=HOURLY',
          enabled         => TRUE,
          comments        => 'Keep 24 hours of ZET vehicle positions'
        );
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Monitor the purge job: state, run counts, last and next run (Zagreb
      time)

      --


      SELECT
        job_name,
        state,
        enabled,
        run_count,
        failure_count,
        CAST(last_start_date AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP(0)) AS last_start_local,
        last_run_duration,
        CAST(next_run_date AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP(0)) AS next_run_local
      FROM
        user_scheduler_jobs
      WHERE
        job_name = 'ZET_PURGE_JOB';

      --

      -- Job runs of the last 24 hours, newest first, with error code and
      message of failed runs

      --


      SELECT
        log_id,
        CAST(actual_start_date AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP(0)) AS started_local,
        run_duration,
        status,
        error#,
        errors
      FROM
        user_scheduler_job_run_details
      WHERE
        job_name = 'ZET_PURGE_JOB'
        AND log_date > SYSTIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '24' HOUR
      ORDER BY
        log_date DESC;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Disable the purge job (uncomment to run)

      -- The job and its run history stay;
      DBMS_SCHEDULER.ENABLE('ZET_PURGE_JOB') switches it on again.

      -- force => TRUE: a run in progress is allowed to finish, no further runs
      start.

      --


      BEGIN
        DBMS_SCHEDULER.DISABLE(name => 'ZET_PURGE_JOB', force => TRUE);
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: |
      --
      -- Stream statistics
      --

      SELECT
        COUNT(*) AS feed_messages,
        MIN(feed_ts) AS first_feed_utc,
        MAX(feed_ts) AS last_feed_utc
      FROM
        zet_feed_raw;

      SELECT
        COUNT(*) AS positions,
        COUNT(DISTINCT vehicle_id) AS vehicles,
        COUNT(DISTINCT route_id) AS routes,
        MIN(observed_at) AS first_utc,
        MAX(observed_at) AS last_utc
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions;
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: |-
      ## 13. Loader Job in the Database

      The loader runs inside the database, so no Python script or external machine is needed. The function `ZET_FEED.POLL` runs every 15 seconds as the scheduler job `ZET_FEED_JOB`:

      1. **Fetch** the feed message with `UTL_HTTP` over HTTPS: about 75 KB of protobuf.
      2. **Decode** the protobuf in PL/SQL. GTFS-Realtime needs only three protobuf wire types: varints (base-128 integers), length-delimited fields (strings and nested messages) and 32-bit floats (`UTL_RAW.CAST_TO_BINARY_FLOAT`). The decoder skips everything except the header and the vehicle positions, and builds the JSON that `ZET_STREAM.INGEST` expects with `JSON_OBJECT_T`.
      3. **Ingest** with `ZET_STREAM.INGEST` (section 6): the same landing table and `MERGE` as the ORDS endpoint. A message whose header timestamp is already in `ZET_FEED_RAW` is skipped, so polling an unchanged feed adds nothing.

      A Python copy of the decoder logic gave the same result as Google's protobuf library for a live message of 8 October 2026 (291 vehicles, 14 on tram 6).

      | | External loader (section 11) | Loader job (this section) |
      |---|---|---|
      | Runs | Outside the database, POSTs to `/zet-ingest/positions` | In the database, as a scheduler job |
      | Needs | Script, protobuf library, OAuth client | Package `ZET_FEED`, job `ZET_FEED_JOB`, network ACL |
      | Stored in `ZET_FEED_RAW` | What the loader sends | Header and vehicle positions; trip updates are skipped |

      **Prerequisite, run once as ADMIN.** Autonomous AI Database allows outbound HTTPS on port 443 to hosts that a network ACL opens. Its default wallet holds the common public root certificates; zet.hr uses a Sectigo certificate. Replace `<SCHEMA>` with the notebook schema:

      ```sql
      BEGIN
        DBMS_NETWORK_ACL_ADMIN.APPEND_HOST_ACE(
          host       => 'zet.hr',
          lower_port => 443,
          upper_port => 443,
          ace        => xs$ace_type(privilege_list => xs$name_list('http'),
                                    principal_name => '<SCHEMA>',
                                    principal_type => xs_acl.ptype_db));
      END;
      /
      -- Only if compiling ZET_FEED reports PLS-00201 for UTL_HTTP:
      GRANT EXECUTE ON UTL_HTTP TO <SCHEMA>;
      ```

      **Every 15 seconds** (`FREQ=SECONDLY;INTERVAL=15`) means 5,760 runs a day, so the job logs failed runs only; `RUN_COUNT` still counts every run. A run never overlaps the previous one, and a 10-second transfer timeout ends a hanging request before the next run is due. The purge job from section 12 keeps the tables at 24 hours.

      References:
      * [GTFS Realtime protocol buffer definition](https://gtfs.org/documentation/realtime/proto/)
      * [Protocol Buffers encoding](https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/encoding/)
      * [Autonomous AI Database: call web services](https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/paas/autonomous-database/serverless/adbsb/call-web-services.html)
      * [UTL_HTTP (26ai)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/arpls/UTL_HTTP.html), [UTL_RAW (26ai)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/arpls/UTL_RAW.html), [DBMS_SCHEDULER (26ai)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/arpls/DBMS_SCHEDULER.html)
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Check the network ACL: one row with PRIVILEGE = HTTP for zet.hr, port
      443

      --


      SELECT host, lower_port, upper_port, privilege, status

      FROM user_host_aces

      WHERE host = 'zet.hr';
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      CREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE zet_feed AS

        -- ZET GTFS-Realtime feed (protobuf)
        c_feed_url CONSTANT VARCHAR2(100) := 'https://zet.hr/gtfs-rt-protobuf';

        -- Fetch one feed message (protobuf) over HTTPS
        FUNCTION fetch_feed (p_url IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT c_feed_url) RETURN BLOB;

        -- Decode a GTFS-RT FeedMessage into the JSON that ZET_STREAM.INGEST expects (header and vehicle positions)
        FUNCTION to_json (p_feed IN BLOB) RETURN CLOB;

        -- Poll once: fetch, decode, ingest. Returns the number of new positions, 0 if the message was loaded before.
        -- The caller commits.
        FUNCTION poll (p_url IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT c_feed_url) RETURN NUMBER;

      END zet_feed;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      CREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY zet_feed AS

        -- Decoder state: the message and the read position (1-based, as in DBMS_LOB)
        g_msg BLOB;
        g_pos PLS_INTEGER;

        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        -- Protobuf wire format (https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/encoding/)
        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

        FUNCTION read_byte RETURN PLS_INTEGER IS
          l_byte PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          l_byte := TO_NUMBER(RAWTOHEX(DBMS_LOB.SUBSTR(g_msg, 1, g_pos)), 'XX');
          g_pos  := g_pos + 1;
          RETURN l_byte;
        END read_byte;

        -- Varint (wire type 0): 7 bits per byte, least significant group first
        FUNCTION read_varint RETURN NUMBER IS
          l_byte  PLS_INTEGER;
          l_value NUMBER := 0;
          l_mult  NUMBER := 1;
        BEGIN
          LOOP
            l_byte  := read_byte;
            l_value := l_value + MOD(l_byte, 128) * l_mult;
            EXIT WHEN l_byte < 128;
            l_mult  := l_mult * 128;
          END LOOP;
          RETURN l_value;
        END read_varint;

        -- Length-delimited UTF-8 string (wire type 2)
        FUNCTION read_string RETURN VARCHAR2 IS
          l_len PLS_INTEGER := read_varint;
          l_str VARCHAR2(32767);
        BEGIN
          IF l_len > 0 THEN
            l_str := UTL_RAW.CAST_TO_VARCHAR2(DBMS_LOB.SUBSTR(g_msg, l_len, g_pos));
            g_pos := g_pos + l_len;
          END IF;
          RETURN l_str;
        END read_string;

        -- 32-bit IEEE 754 float, little-endian (wire type 5)
        FUNCTION read_float RETURN NUMBER IS
          l_value BINARY_FLOAT;
        BEGIN
          l_value := UTL_RAW.CAST_TO_BINARY_FLOAT(DBMS_LOB.SUBSTR(g_msg, 4, g_pos), UTL_RAW.LITTLE_ENDIAN);
          g_pos   := g_pos + 4;
          RETURN ROUND(TO_NUMBER(l_value), 6);
        END read_float;

        -- Skip a field the decoder doesn't need
        PROCEDURE skip_field (p_wire IN PLS_INTEGER) IS
          l_len NUMBER;
        BEGIN
          CASE p_wire
            WHEN 0 THEN l_len := read_varint;                         -- varint
            WHEN 1 THEN g_pos := g_pos + 8;                           -- 64-bit
            WHEN 2 THEN l_len := read_varint;                         -- length-delimited
                        g_pos := g_pos + l_len;
            WHEN 5 THEN g_pos := g_pos + 4;                           -- 32-bit
            ELSE RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20802, 'Unsupported protobuf wire type ' || p_wire || ' at byte ' || g_pos);
          END CASE;
        END skip_field;

        -- Next field before p_end: field number and wire type; FALSE at the end of the (embedded) message
        FUNCTION next_field (p_end IN PLS_INTEGER, p_field OUT PLS_INTEGER, p_wire OUT PLS_INTEGER) RETURN BOOLEAN IS
          l_key NUMBER;
        BEGIN
          IF g_pos >= p_end THEN
            RETURN FALSE;
          END IF;
          l_key   := read_varint;
          p_field := TRUNC(l_key / 8);
          p_wire  := MOD(l_key, 8);
          RETURN TRUE;
        END next_field;

        -- End position of the embedded message that starts here (its length comes first)
        FUNCTION sub_end RETURN PLS_INTEGER IS
          l_len NUMBER := read_varint;
        BEGIN
          RETURN g_pos + l_len;
        END sub_end;

        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        -- GTFS-Realtime messages (field numbers: https://gtfs.org/documentation/realtime/proto/)
        -- Output: Protocol Buffers JSON mapping (camelCase names, uint64 as string)
        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

        -- TripDescriptor
        FUNCTION decode_trip (p_end IN PLS_INTEGER) RETURN JSON_OBJECT_T IS
          l_obj   JSON_OBJECT_T := JSON_OBJECT_T();
          l_field PLS_INTEGER;
          l_wire  PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          WHILE next_field(p_end, l_field, l_wire) LOOP
            CASE
              WHEN l_field = 1 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('tripId',      read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 2 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('startTime',   read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 3 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('startDate',   read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 5 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('routeId',     read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 6 AND l_wire = 0 THEN l_obj.put('directionId', read_varint);
              ELSE skip_field(l_wire);
            END CASE;
          END LOOP;
          RETURN l_obj;
        END decode_trip;

        -- Position
        FUNCTION decode_position (p_end IN PLS_INTEGER) RETURN JSON_OBJECT_T IS
          l_obj   JSON_OBJECT_T := JSON_OBJECT_T();
          l_field PLS_INTEGER;
          l_wire  PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          WHILE next_field(p_end, l_field, l_wire) LOOP
            CASE
              WHEN l_field = 1 AND l_wire = 5 THEN l_obj.put('latitude',  read_float);
              WHEN l_field = 2 AND l_wire = 5 THEN l_obj.put('longitude', read_float);
              WHEN l_field = 3 AND l_wire = 5 THEN l_obj.put('bearing',   read_float);
              WHEN l_field = 5 AND l_wire = 5 THEN l_obj.put('speed',     read_float);
              ELSE skip_field(l_wire);
            END CASE;
          END LOOP;
          RETURN l_obj;
        END decode_position;

        -- VehicleDescriptor
        FUNCTION decode_descriptor (p_end IN PLS_INTEGER) RETURN JSON_OBJECT_T IS
          l_obj   JSON_OBJECT_T := JSON_OBJECT_T();
          l_field PLS_INTEGER;
          l_wire  PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          WHILE next_field(p_end, l_field, l_wire) LOOP
            CASE
              WHEN l_field = 1 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('id',           read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 2 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('label',        read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 3 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('licensePlate', read_string);
              ELSE skip_field(l_wire);
            END CASE;
          END LOOP;
          RETURN l_obj;
        END decode_descriptor;

        -- VehiclePosition
        FUNCTION decode_vehicle (p_end IN PLS_INTEGER) RETURN JSON_OBJECT_T IS
          l_obj   JSON_OBJECT_T := JSON_OBJECT_T();
          l_field PLS_INTEGER;
          l_wire  PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          WHILE next_field(p_end, l_field, l_wire) LOOP
            CASE
              WHEN l_field = 1 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('trip',                decode_trip(sub_end));
              WHEN l_field = 2 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('position',            decode_position(sub_end));
              WHEN l_field = 3 AND l_wire = 0 THEN l_obj.put('currentStopSequence', read_varint);
              WHEN l_field = 5 AND l_wire = 0 THEN l_obj.put('timestamp',           TO_CHAR(read_varint));
              WHEN l_field = 7 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('stopId',              read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 8 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_obj.put('vehicle',             decode_descriptor(sub_end));
              ELSE skip_field(l_wire);
            END CASE;
          END LOOP;
          RETURN l_obj;
        END decode_vehicle;

        -- FeedEntity: only entities with a vehicle position; trip updates and alerts are skipped (NULL)
        FUNCTION decode_entity (p_end IN PLS_INTEGER) RETURN JSON_OBJECT_T IS
          l_obj     JSON_OBJECT_T;
          l_id      VARCHAR2(200);
          l_vehicle JSON_OBJECT_T;
          l_field   PLS_INTEGER;
          l_wire    PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          WHILE next_field(p_end, l_field, l_wire) LOOP
            CASE
              WHEN l_field = 1 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_id      := read_string;
              WHEN l_field = 4 AND l_wire = 2 THEN l_vehicle := decode_vehicle(sub_end);
              ELSE skip_field(l_wire);
            END CASE;
          END LOOP;
          IF l_vehicle IS NOT NULL THEN
            l_obj := JSON_OBJECT_T();
            l_obj.put('id', l_id);
            l_obj.put('vehicle', l_vehicle);
          END IF;
          RETURN l_obj;
        END decode_entity;

        -- FeedHeader; p_epoch returns the header timestamp (POSIX seconds, UTC)
        FUNCTION decode_header (p_end IN PLS_INTEGER, p_epoch OUT NUMBER) RETURN JSON_OBJECT_T IS
          l_obj   JSON_OBJECT_T := JSON_OBJECT_T();
          l_field PLS_INTEGER;
          l_wire  PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          WHILE next_field(p_end, l_field, l_wire) LOOP
            CASE
              WHEN l_field = 1 AND l_wire = 2 THEN
                l_obj.put('gtfsRealtimeVersion', read_string);
              WHEN l_field = 2 AND l_wire = 0 THEN
                l_obj.put('incrementality', CASE read_varint WHEN 0 THEN 'FULL_DATASET' ELSE 'DIFFERENTIAL' END);
              WHEN l_field = 3 AND l_wire = 0 THEN
                p_epoch := read_varint;
                l_obj.put('timestamp', TO_CHAR(p_epoch));
              ELSE
                skip_field(l_wire);
            END CASE;
          END LOOP;
          RETURN l_obj;
        END decode_header;

        -- FeedMessage
        PROCEDURE decode (p_feed IN BLOB, p_json OUT CLOB, p_epoch OUT NUMBER) IS
          l_root     JSON_OBJECT_T := JSON_OBJECT_T();
          l_entities JSON_ARRAY_T  := JSON_ARRAY_T();
          l_entity   JSON_OBJECT_T;
          l_end      PLS_INTEGER;
          l_field    PLS_INTEGER;
          l_wire     PLS_INTEGER;
        BEGIN
          g_msg := p_feed;
          g_pos := 1;
          l_end := DBMS_LOB.GETLENGTH(p_feed) + 1;
          WHILE next_field(l_end, l_field, l_wire) LOOP
            CASE
              WHEN l_field = 1 AND l_wire = 2 THEN
                l_root.put('header', decode_header(sub_end, p_epoch));
              WHEN l_field = 2 AND l_wire = 2 THEN
                l_entity := decode_entity(sub_end);
                IF l_entity IS NOT NULL THEN
                  l_entities.append(l_entity);
                END IF;
              ELSE
                skip_field(l_wire);
            END CASE;
          END LOOP;
          l_root.put('entity', l_entities);
          p_json := l_root.to_clob;
          IF DBMS_LOB.ISTEMPORARY(g_msg) = 1 THEN
            DBMS_LOB.FREETEMPORARY(g_msg);                            -- the copy made by the assignment above
          END IF;
        END decode;

        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        -- Public
        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

        FUNCTION fetch_feed (p_url IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT c_feed_url) RETURN BLOB IS
          l_req  UTL_HTTP.REQ;
          l_resp UTL_HTTP.RESP;
          l_raw  RAW(32767);
          l_feed BLOB;
        BEGIN
          UTL_HTTP.SET_DETAILED_EXCP_SUPPORT(TRUE);                   -- raises END_OF_BODY at the end of the response
          UTL_HTTP.SET_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT(10);                          -- seconds; less than the 15-second poll interval
          l_req := UTL_HTTP.BEGIN_REQUEST(p_url, 'GET', 'HTTP/1.1');
          UTL_HTTP.SET_HEADER(l_req, 'Accept', 'application/x-protobuf');
          UTL_HTTP.SET_HEADER(l_req, 'User-Agent', 'zet-feed-loader (Oracle AI Database)');
          l_resp := UTL_HTTP.GET_RESPONSE(l_req);
          IF l_resp.status_code <> UTL_HTTP.HTTP_OK THEN
            RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20801, 'Feed request failed: HTTP ' || l_resp.status_code || ' ' || l_resp.reason_phrase);
          END IF;
          DBMS_LOB.CREATETEMPORARY(l_feed, TRUE);
          BEGIN
            LOOP
              UTL_HTTP.READ_RAW(l_resp, l_raw, 32767);
              DBMS_LOB.WRITEAPPEND(l_feed, UTL_RAW.LENGTH(l_raw), l_raw);
            END LOOP;
          EXCEPTION
            WHEN UTL_HTTP.END_OF_BODY THEN
              UTL_HTTP.END_RESPONSE(l_resp);
          END;
          RETURN l_feed;
        EXCEPTION
          WHEN OTHERS THEN
            BEGIN
              UTL_HTTP.END_RESPONSE(l_resp);                          -- close the connection if still open
            EXCEPTION
              WHEN OTHERS THEN NULL;
            END;
            RAISE;
        END fetch_feed;

        FUNCTION to_json (p_feed IN BLOB) RETURN CLOB IS
          l_json  CLOB;
          l_epoch NUMBER;
        BEGIN
          decode(p_feed, l_json, l_epoch);
          RETURN l_json;
        END to_json;

        FUNCTION poll (p_url IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT c_feed_url) RETURN NUMBER IS
          l_feed    BLOB;
          l_json    CLOB;
          l_epoch   NUMBER;
          l_feed_ts TIMESTAMP;
          l_loaded  PLS_INTEGER;
          l_feed_id NUMBER;
          l_rows    NUMBER := 0;
        BEGIN
          l_feed := fetch_feed(p_url);
          decode(l_feed, l_json, l_epoch);
          IF l_epoch IS NULL THEN
            RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20803, 'Feed message without header timestamp');
          END IF;
          l_feed_ts := TIMESTAMP '1970-01-01 00:00:00' + NUMTODSINTERVAL(l_epoch, 'SECOND');

          -- Skip a message that was loaded before (the feed hasn't changed since the last poll)
          SELECT COUNT(*)
          INTO   l_loaded
          FROM   zet_feed_raw
          WHERE  received_date >= CAST(SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP) - INTERVAL '1' HOUR AS DATE)   -- recent partitions only
          AND    feed_ts = l_feed_ts;

          IF l_loaded = 0 THEN
            zet_stream.ingest(p_payload => l_json, p_feed_id => l_feed_id, p_rows => l_rows);
          END IF;

          DBMS_LOB.FREETEMPORARY(l_feed);
          DBMS_LOB.FREETEMPORARY(l_json);
          RETURN l_rows;
        END poll;

      END zet_feed;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Test 1: fetch and decode one message in PL/SQL, without storing it

      --


      DECLARE
        l_doc      JSON_OBJECT_T := JSON_OBJECT_T.parse(zet_feed.to_json(zet_feed.fetch_feed));
        l_entities JSON_ARRAY_T  := l_doc.get_array('entity');
      BEGIN
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('header:   ' || l_doc.get_object('header').to_string);
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('vehicles: ' || l_entities.get_size);
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('first:    ' || l_entities.get(0).to_string);
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Test 2: one poll (fetch, decode, ingest). Run it again right away: an
      unchanged feed adds 0 positions.

      --


      DECLARE
        l_rows NUMBER;
      BEGIN
        l_rows := zet_feed.poll;
        COMMIT;
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('new positions: ' || l_rows);
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Loader job: poll the feed every 15 seconds (5,760 runs a day, so only
      failed runs are logged)

      --


      BEGIN
        DBMS_SCHEDULER.CREATE_JOB(
          job_name        => 'ZET_FEED_JOB',
          job_type        => 'PLSQL_BLOCK',
          job_action      => 'DECLARE l_rows NUMBER; BEGIN l_rows := zet_feed.poll; COMMIT; END;',
          start_date      => SYSTIMESTAMP,
          repeat_interval => 'FREQ=SECONDLY;INTERVAL=15',
          enabled         => FALSE,
          comments        => 'Load the ZET GTFS-Realtime feed every 15 seconds'
        );
        DBMS_SCHEDULER.SET_ATTRIBUTE(
          name      => 'ZET_FEED_JOB',
          attribute => 'logging_level',
          value     => DBMS_SCHEDULER.LOGGING_FAILED_RUNS
        );
        DBMS_SCHEDULER.ENABLE('ZET_FEED_JOB');
      END;

      /
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: >
      --

      -- Monitor the loader job: state, run counts, last and next run (Zagreb
      time)

      --


      SELECT
        job_name,
        state,
        enabled,
        run_count,
        failure_count,
        CAST(last_start_date AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP(0)) AS last_start_local,
        last_run_duration,
        CAST(next_run_date AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP(0)) AS next_run_local
      FROM
        user_scheduler_jobs
      WHERE
        job_name = 'ZET_FEED_JOB';

      --

      -- Failed runs of the last 24 hours, newest first, with error code and
      message (successful runs are not logged)

      --


      SELECT
        log_id,
        CAST(actual_start_date AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP(0)) AS started_local,
        run_duration,
        status,
        error#,
        errors
      FROM
        user_scheduler_job_run_details
      WHERE
        job_name = 'ZET_FEED_JOB'
        AND log_date > SYSTIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '24' HOUR
      ORDER BY
        log_date DESC;

      --

      -- Is data arriving? Feed messages and positions of the last 10 minutes

      --


      SELECT
        COUNT(*) AS feed_messages,
        CAST(FROM_TZ(MAX(feed_ts), 'UTC') AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Zagreb' AS TIMESTAMP(0)) AS last_feed_local
      FROM
        zet_feed_raw
      WHERE
        received_date >= CAST(SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP) - INTERVAL '10' MINUTE AS DATE);

      SELECT
        COUNT(*) AS positions,
        COUNT(DISTINCT vehicle_id) AS vehicles,
        COUNT(DISTINCT route_id) AS routes
      FROM
        zet_vehicle_positions
      WHERE
        observed_date >= CAST(SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP) - INTERVAL '10' MINUTE AS DATE)
        AND observed_at >= SYS_EXTRACT_UTC(SYSTIMESTAMP) - INTERVAL '10' MINUTE;
    languageId: oracle-sql
